        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Ryan Budney, JSJ-decompositions of knot and link complements in the 3-sphere",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0506523",
              "locator": "§§1, 2, and 4; satellite patterns, companions, and companionship graphs"
            },
            {
              "title": "Allen Hatcher, Notes on Basic 3-Manifold Topology",
              "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/3M/3M.pdf",
              "locator": "§1.2, torus decomposition conventions, printed pp.11–23"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "Assume countable choice for the chosen smooth knot-exterior data. Let V=S^1×D^2 be the standard oriented solid torus, with oriented core S^1×{0}. A pattern is a smooth oriented knot P contained in the interior of V, together with a chosen closed tubular neighbourhood N(P) contained in the interior of V. Its winding number w(P) is the integer by which the homology class [P] maps to H_1(V;Z)≅Z under the isomorphism that sends the oriented core to 1. The pattern exterior is X_P=V\\operatorname{int}N(P); its outer torus is ∂V and its inner torus is ∂N(P). A companion is a smooth oriented knot K⊂S^3 with a specified orientation-preserving tubular embedding e:V→S^3 that sends the core to K and identifies the solid-torus meridian and product longitude with the positive meridian and preferred longitude of K. The satellite knot P(K) is e(P), with chosen exterior E_{P(K)}=S^3\\operatorname{int}e(N(P)); the image e(∂V) is its companion torus. The outer torus is incompressible when it bounds no properly embedded disk in X_P with essential boundary on ∂V. It is product-parallel to the inner torus when X_P is a product T^2×[0,1] with those tori as its ends. This definition alone asserts neither incompressibility nor essentiality of the companion torus."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-incompressibility-seifert-fibration-and-jsj-system",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Incompressibility, Seifert fibrations, and JSJ systems",
        "deps": [
