          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Allen Hatcher, Notes on Basic 3-Manifold Topology",
              "url": "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/3M/3M.pdf",
              "locator": "§1.2, definitions preceding Theorem 1.9 and Lemma 1.10, printed pp.11–16"
            },
            {
              "title": "Walter Neumann and Gadde Swarup, Canonical decompositions of 3-manifolds",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/9712227",
              "locator": "§§1–4, canonical surfaces and torus-boundary JSJ conventions"
            }
          ]
        },
        "statement": "For a compact smooth 3-manifold M, a properly embedded surface is two-sided if it has a product neighbourhood F×[-1,1] in M. A compressing disk for a two-sided surface F is an embedded disk D⊂M with int(D)∩F=∅ and ∂D an essential simple closed curve in F; F is incompressible if it has no compressing disk. A boundary-compressing disk is an embedded disk D with ∂D=α∪β, where α=D∩F is an arc not cutting off a disk in F and β=D∩∂M, and int(D) misses F∪∂M; F is boundary-incompressible if there is no such disk. A surface is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product with a subsurface of ∂M, and is essential if it is incompressible, boundary-incompressible, and not boundary-parallel. M is irreducible if every embedded 2-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded 3-ball in M. An incompressible torus is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product T^2×[0,1] with a component of ∂M; M is atoroidal if every incompressible torus is boundary-parallel. A Seifert fibration is a decomposition into circles with local models given by fibered solid tori; in the model (D^2×S^1)/⟨(z,w)↦(e^{2πi/q}z,e^{2πip/q}w)⟩, gcd(p,q)=1, the core fiber has multiplicity q. Fibers of multiplicity 1 are regular and those of multiplicity greater than 1 are exceptional. A finite disjoint family of incompressible tori is a JSJ system if every component obtained by cutting M along the family is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. It is inclusion-minimal if no proper subfamily is a JSJ system. Atoroidal does not mean hyperbolic."
      },
